# Provenance for s4cov_crop_format_2026-08-03.tsv
# (KB-28's crop axis at the formats its residual named as unmeasured.)

base commit      : 1f366a9 (origin/main) + this session's KB-35 fix
host             : Darwin arm64 / Apple M4 Pro
profile          : test-fast (opt-level 3, debug-assertions + overflow-checks ON)
dispatch         : default (archmage SIMD tokens live)
conformance dir  : AOM_CONFORMANCE_DIR=<primary checkout>/conformance/data
wall             : 377 s for the four arms (60 encode PAIRS)

command          : cargo test --profile test-fast -p zenav1-aom-bench \
                     --test s4cov_crop_format_axis -- --ignored --nocapture --test-threads=1

THE TWO CROPS (both from `kb28_crop_dims::CELLS`)
  474x480  mi-aligned 480x480 — crop min-dim 474 < 480 <= mi min-dim 480
  714x720  mi-aligned 720x720 — crop min-dim 714 < 720 <= mi min-dim 720
  `av1_get_MBs` (alloccommon.c:30-33) aligns to 8 px, so the gap is up to 7 px.

RESULT BY ARM
  chroma formats (mono / 4:4:4 / 4:2:2 x 2 crops x cpu 1..6)   36/36 MATCH
  SB128        (--sb-size=128 x 2 crops x cpu 1..6)            12/12 MATCH
  bd10         (2 crops + 2 SB-exact controls x cpu {0, 7})     8/8  MATCH
  cpu 0        (4:2:0 x 2 crops)                                2/2  MATCH
  cpu 0        (monochrome x 2 crops + 2 SB-exact controls)     0/4  DIVERGE

THE MONOCHROME cpu-0 ROWS ARE NOT THIS AXIS'S FINDING
  They are `s4cov_partial_sb_axis::MONO_S0_OPEN` — the pre-existing
  (monochrome, base_qindex 96, speed 0) near-tie, whose own localizer
  (`mono_speed0_size_qindex_localize`) reduces it to a SINGLE 64x64
  superblock, i.e. to a frame with no straddle at all. Measured here, not
  argued: the SB-exact monochrome controls at the crops' own mi-aligned
  extents (480x480 -211 B, 720x720 +105 B) diverge too, where KB-28's fix
  is a literal no-op. What this run DOES add is the shape: the class was
  pinned at 132x132 / 192x192 / 196x196 / 256x256 and now also reaches
  474x480 / 480x480 / 714x720 / 720x720, i.e. it is size-independent.

A PREDICATE THAT WAS WRONG, AND WAS REFUTED BY THE FIRST RUN
  The SB128 arm originally asserted 474x480 would be byte-identical to its
  SB64 encode, from `av1_select_sb_size`'s `AOMMIN(w,h) <= 480 -> BLOCK_64X64`
  rule (the rule KB-34's entry quotes). That rule lives in the
  AOM_SUPERBLOCK_SIZE_DYNAMIC branch; an explicit --sb-size=128 returns
  BLOCK_128X128 from the top of the function (encoder_utils.c:961-963). Both
  crops are genuine SB128, and the C stream changes on 12/12 rows.

APPENDED 2026-08-04 — the bd12 arm (rows at the tail of the TSV)
  8/8 byte-exact in 53 s: 474x480 and 714x720 plus their SB-exact controls at
  their own mi-aligned extents (480x480, 720x720), at cpu {0, 7}. Re-run
  byte-exact under AOM_FORCE_SCALAR=1. Bite proof: making
  `SbEncodeEnv::frame_min_dim()` return the MI extent (KB-28's root) fails it
  on `b12crop 474x480 cpu0` (+7 B) with all four controls byte-exact — a
  narrow bite (1 of 4 crop rows), stated as narrow rather than dressed up.

